  "updated": "2026-11-21T23:20:27.346130+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure is passing through the region this afternoon, with\na strong upper level ridge aloft. As with the last few days,\ntemperatures are well above average for highs and that will \nagain be the case Sunday with upper 50s-lower 60s. What is\ndifferent, is that a shield of high clouds will begin to stream\ninto the region tonight well ahead of a coastal low, as a\nsoutherly return flow also gets going on the back side of the\ndeparting surface high. So unlike the last few frosty mornings,\ntonight should be relatively milder with lows mostly in the 40s.\nThe Mohawk Valley however could still radiate into the upper \n30s before the clouds thicken enough. Then for Sunday itself,\ntop-down moistening will occur so there will be plenty of \nclouds and an eventual late day encroachment of rain chances.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA coastal low will bring some rain mainly to eastern parts of\nour area late Sunday through Sunday night, followed by a cold \nfrontal passage Monday with very limited moisture. Models have\ntrended more compact and farther east with the track of the \nlow, to the point that portions of the Finger Lakes to Syracuse\nareas may not even get a drop of needed rain despite the \nongoing moderate drought conditions in that part of our area. \nMeanwhile, farther east, there is still a decent likelihood of \nat least some rain towards the Poconos and Catskills though with\nexpected amounts lowered into the one to two thirds of an inch \nrange. Probabilities of exceeding an inch have dropped to only \n30-40 percent in our Poconos-Catskills zones, and well below 20\npercent northwest of an I-88 to Towanda PA axis. Indeed, it \nremains possible that even the Poconos-Catskills could get \nbarely skimmed by the outer edge of the low and thus forecast \nrain amounts could still drop some more.\n\nRegardless of the exact track, the coastal low quickly moves \nnortheast Monday, with a mostly dry cold frontal passage \noccurring right behind it. Very minor mixed rain-snow lake \neffect showers could sneak into parts of Central NY Monday \nnight, but moisture will be too shallow for it to amount to \nanything of significance. What will occur, however, is a \nreintroduction to a more seasonable airmass, placing highs in \nthe mid 30s-mid 40s range Tuesday and Wednesday. Tuesday\nnight could actually be quite chilly with high pressure passing\noverhead with good radiational cooling of a dry air mass; \nforecast lows are upper teens-mid 20s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nPotential for a stronger low pressure system remains for late \nthis week, though how impactful it could be is quite uncertain, \nif indeed at all. Models continue to show plenty of spread, with\ngood confidence in the existence of a stronger low within the \ngeneral region late this week moving in around Thanksgiving, yet\nvery low confidence in the tracks/locations and details. The \noverall trend is also somewhat slower and warmer, which if so \nwould suggest a wetter scenario versus a wintry one. We will \ncontinue to monitor trends as there is plenty of uncertainty \nand thus a lot to sort out. Precipitation chances in general are\nclimbing, peaking at a 50-70 percent chance range late \nThanksgiving through Thursday night-early Friday. Even if \nprecipitation turns out to be likely, the type remains quite \nuncertain as the depth of the trough digging into the central US\nhas a significant amount of spread in the model ensembles. \nProbability for even minor winter weather impacts are currently \nonly in the 10-40 percent range by Thursday night, highest \ngenerally in elevated terrain like the Catskills, and even less \nthan that towards the Wyoming Valley PA.",
